1
00:00:00,152 --> 00:00:06,979
Tastebuds keeps a taste profile. This is how you add an entry, and how you move it once it shapes your work.

2
00:00:07,596 --> 00:00:13,719
The app runs in your browser. A profile is one file on your disk, so there is no account.

3
00:00:13,785 --> 00:00:18,159
Open the example. It is the profile of Rosa, a home cook.

4
00:00:18,206 --> 00:00:28,361
A profile keeps two lists apart. Inspirations gave something to her cooking, and she credits each one. Taste references are cooks whose taste she watches.

5
00:00:28,421 --> 00:00:31,429
Add a taste reference: a bakery she likes.

6
00:00:31,492 --> 00:00:35,034
Give it a name, a kind, and a link to its own site.

7
00:00:35,089 --> 00:00:37,158
Tags say what you see in it.

8
00:00:37,174 --> 00:00:39,819
Save, and it joins the taste you watch.

9
00:00:39,855 --> 00:00:46,917
Months later, your cookbook borrows the way it folds butter into its morning buns. Now it has shaped your work.

10
00:00:46,971 --> 00:00:49,147
So move it to inspirations.

11
00:00:49,170 --> 00:00:53,949
The app asks two things: what it gave, and which work of yours credits it.

12
00:00:54,091 --> 00:00:57,846
Save. The entry moves across, with its credit beside it.

13
00:00:57,897 --> 00:01:04,148
A taste reference never shows as an influence. An inspiration always names what it gave.

14
00:01:04,182 --> 00:01:07,489
Then save the file, or export it for a web page.

15
00:01:08,069 --> 00:01:11,589
Tastebuds is open source. The guide has the rest.
